WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.180
In February, 2026, an auction changed

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.440
how the AI boom is measured.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.850
PJM, the grid operator for 13

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.740
American states, held its annual capacity

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:11.520
auction.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:12.810
The price cleared at a record

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.060
$329 per megawatt day, the

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.550
highest ever allowed, because, for the

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.080
first time, the grid could not

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.560
fully guarantee the power it promised.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.960
Here is the part that surprised

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:24.570
people.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.540
The auction monitor says data centers

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.030
drove about $6.3 billion of that

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.710
cost, 38% of the entire bill.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.020
That is the question we are

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.060
going to unpack.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.310
Can the world's grids grow as

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.160
quickly as AI's demand for electricity?

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.270
The short answer is that the

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.400
constraint on AI has moved.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.760
It used to be chips.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.320
Now it is cables.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.280
Start with the load itself.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:47.860
A modern AI data center is

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.820
a building filled with thousands of

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.630
servers running around the clock, with

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.880
liquid cooling and backup power stacked

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:54.480
on top.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.930
One large campus can draw as

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.890
much electricity as a small city

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.880
around a gigawatt or more.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.160
Northern Virginia, the world's largest data

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.430
center cluster, now consumes more power

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.160
than some small countries.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:07.690
The servers that answer your prompts

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.840
are, quite literally, a city-sized machine.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.640
This is the definition we need.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:13.840
A data center is not a

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:14.720
building.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.280
It is a demand curve, and

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.340
that demand curve is climbing fast.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.710
The International Energy Agency now projects

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.010
global data center electricity use will

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.620
roughly double by 2030, from around

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.720
500 terawatt hours to about 950,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.250
with AI-specific capacity tripling over the

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.280
same period.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.080
That is not a rounding error.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.330
That is like adding a second

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.760
Japan to the world's electricity system.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.400
Now follow the chain.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.970
The AI stack runs chips, memory,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.960
storage, and cooling, and every layer

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.000
is electricity.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.660
The bottleneck is no longer the

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:47.460
chip plant.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.130
It is the grid connection, the

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.680
transformer, the transmission line that brings

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:52.920
power to the machine.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.580
Here is where the market comes

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:54.920
in.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.490
In the United States, the queue

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.920
of projects waiting to connect to

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.160
the grid holds roughly 2,600 gigawatts

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:02.220
of requests.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.490
Median weights have stretched past five

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.100
years.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.100
A data center cannot wait five

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:08.700
years, so the hyperscalers are going

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:09.680
around the queue.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.670
Microsoft struck a deal to restart

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.080
Three Mile Island.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.640
Amazon backed Xenergies' small modular reactors.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.740
Google signed up Kairos, and meta

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:20.160
chose Terra Power.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.660
When the grid cannot deliver fast

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.750
enough, the biggest buyers are building

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.160
their own power plants.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.960
That is why markets care.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.200
Electricity is becoming the binding input

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:30.940
for the most valuable companies on

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.280
Earth, and whoever controls power supply

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.080
is capturing the toll booth.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.880
So what is the honest uncertainty?

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.880
Nuclear is clean and steady, but

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.520
slow and expensive.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.980
Gas is fast, but it is

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.360
a fossil fuel and it is

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.160
pricey.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.260
Renewables are getting cheaper, but they

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.720
need land, transmission, and storage.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.720
No single answer can scale fast

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:51.760
enough on its own.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.080
The signal to watch is not

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.440
the headline number.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.520
It is the behavior underneath it.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.810
Watch whether utilities can actually connect

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.810
data centers without breaking the grid

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:01.740
for everyone else.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.260
Watch whether the new nuclear and

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.340
gas deals come online on time.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.440
Watch whether efficiency gains can slow

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.140
the curve because AI can use

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.160
less electricity per task, but it

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.840
keeps finding more tasks.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.980
The AI race was about models,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.320
then it was about chips.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.160
Now it is about megawatts.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.250
The grid was built for a

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.670
world where power grew a few

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.660
percent a year.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.970
AI wants a world where it

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.280
can grow on demand.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.800
That is the tension.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.040
And in 2026, it has a

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.370
price tag, a record auction, a

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.610
2,600 gigawatt queue, and a race

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.130
to build the power plants that

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.260
will decide who gets to compute.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.700
The question for the next phase

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.540
of the AI economy is not

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:42.520
whether the models get better.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:43.750
It is whether the lights stay

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:44.860
on while they do.
